const pptxgen=require('pptxgenjs'),fs=require('fs'),path=require('path');
const p=new pptxgen(); p.layout='LAYOUT_WIDE';p.author='Daniel Ortiz-Barrientos and Mark Cooper';p.title='How much fitness variation can a straight line describe?';p.lang='en-AU';
const fg='203A42',teal='226C62',muted='52666F',paper='FCFCF8';
const slides=[
['01 · The question','How much fitness variation can a straight line describe?','The same fitness curve can give different answers in two populations.','Compare the outcome at each breeding value with the frequency of that value.','fig02_two_populations','Start with variation in reproductive output. Ask students what they would need to know about each plant and about the population. Refer to the teaching notes, pages 1–2.'],
['02 · The outcome','Average offspring counts before taking logs','Two plants with the same breeding value leave 0 and 4 surviving offspring. Their mean is 2.','A log of that mean exists. The average of their individual log counts does not.','fig01_offspring_counts','A breeding value sums average allelic effects on a trait in a specified population. The individual outcome can vary at the same breeding value. The plot shows sample means, not the full population curve.'],
['03 · One surface, two populations','Which breeding values occur most often?','Both populations have log expected output y = x − x²/2. Their means agree. Only their variances differ.','Read the density panel before comparing the fitted lines.','fig02_two_populations','The tangent at zero is y=x. The population regression line has slope 1 and intercept −G/2. The intercept changes because the mean quadratic contribution changes.'],
['04 · Variance calculation','Slope contributes one factor of G; curvature contributes two','For a normal breeding value centred at zero, L = bX and Q = HX²/2. Symmetry gives Cov(L,Q) = 0.','Vlin = b²G\\nVquad = H²G²/2','', 'Ask for the variance of X². E[X⁴] − E[X²]² equals 3G² − G² = 2G² for a normal distribution. Symmetry alone does not give that fourth moment.'],
['05 · Population regression','A larger share can accompany less linear variance','At G = 1, the line accounts for 2/3 of the variance. At G = 1/4, it accounts for 8/9.','The linear variance fell fourfold. The quadratic variance fell sixteenfold.','fig02_two_populations','State the target: population regression of the quadratic log conditional mean on breeding value, with an intercept. At zero slope and positive quadratic variance the regression R² is zero, but correlation with a constant is undefined.'],
['06 · A selection prediction','Does a 97% fit bound the error in selection?','In the paper’s example, G = 1, slope b = 2 and curvature H = −1/2. The linear share is 4/(4 + 1/8).','Before showing the shift, ask which quantity selection uses as its weight.','', 'The share is 32/33, about 97%. The linear fit minimises squared error in log expected output. Selection weights by expected reproductive output on its original scale.'],
['07 · The parental mean','The exact shift is 4/3; ignoring curvature gives 2','Multiply the original normal density by expected output. Completing the square gives a new mean of 4/3.','(2 − 4/3)/(4/3) = 50% overestimate','fig03_parental_shift','The denominator is the exact shift. Neither calculation predicts the offspring mean without transmission assumptions. This is a good stopping point for a 35-minute class.'],
];
const figDir=path.join(__dirname,'figures');
